<h2>The contract and the impasse</h2>
<p>The petitioner contractor was engaged under a Letter of Acceptance of January 2026 for construction of a new academic block and extension of the science block at Shivaji College, University of Delhi, with RITES Limited as the employer. Disputes over dues followed. The contractor invoked the escalation route in Clause 25 — the Engineer-in-Charge first, the Appellate Authority next — and met silence at both rungs. When it then petitioned under Section 11(6) for appointment of a sole arbitrator, RITES raised the two standard shields: non-exhaustion of the pre-arbitral mechanism, and accord and satisfaction of all legitimate dues.</p>

<h2>How each objection fared</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>Pre-arbitral ladder</strong><p>Invoked by the contractor; ignored by the employer\'s own designated authorities. Held: an unanswered escalation cannot be wielded as a bar — the petitioner was entitled to proceed to arbitration.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>Accord and satisfaction</strong><p>A merits defence going to discharge of the contract, not to the existence of the arbitration agreement. Held: reserved wholly for the arbitrator, who will decide it in accordance with law.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The doctrinal line from Duro Felguera to Krish Spinning</h2>
<p>The judgment strings together a decade of referral-stage law. Section 11(6-A) reduced the court\'s inquiry to the existence of the arbitration agreement; <em>Duro Felguera</em> and <em>Mayavati Trading</em> enforced that minimalism; the seven-judge decision in <em>In Re: Interplay</em> grounded it in kompetenz-kompetenz; and <em>SBI General Insurance v. Krish Spinning</em> applied it to the discharge context, holding that a contested full-and-final settlement is itself a dispute the clause survives to govern. Later decisions extended the principle to varied settlement scenarios. The referral court\'s role, in short, is a turnstile, not a tribunal — and this petition passed through it.</p>

<h2>Practical architecture of the appointment</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>The order carries the standard protective fittings of modern Section 11 practice: a named sole arbitrator of technical background (a retired CPWD Special Director General), fee regulation under the Fourth Schedule to prevent unilateral fee disputes, a compulsory Section 12 disclosure of independence before entering the reference, liberty to the respondent to raise every defence including discharge, and an express invitation to counterclaim. Contractors should note the sequencing discipline the petitioner displayed — documented invocation of each escalation rung, then a notice invoking arbitration — because it is that paper trail which dissolved the exhaustion objection.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Section 11 petitions for contracts with a Delhi seat lie to the Delhi High Court, and limitation for invoking arbitration runs from the cause of action, not from the end of settlement talks. Public-sector standard forms frequently contain multi-tier clauses; parties should calendar each tier\'s response window and treat silence as a trigger, not an obstacle.</p>
</div>
